@database "ask_user"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: ask_user.guide 1.0 (30.09.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "Ask User"
@{h1}Ask User

Ask User lets an agent stop and ask the person watching the run a question
-- which of two readings of the request is meant, which option they prefer,
whether to go ahead with a step that is hard to undo. The question appears
in the web chat under the run, with up to four options to click and a field
for an answer in one's own words -- or in @{tt}agent-cli chat@{utt}, the
options numbered, answered by the next line typed; the answer goes back to
the model as the tool's result. A run nobody watches -- an API call, a job,
agent-run, a one-shot agent-cli, a closed tab -- is never asked: the tool
answers at once that nobody can answer, and the model decides itself and
says what it assumed. The plugin has one tool and no panel.

@{code}
For users
  @{"  Answering a question  " link answering}
  @{"  Who is asked, and when not  " link attended}

For agents and developers
  @{"  The tool  " link tool}
  @{"  Waiting, and what ends it  " link waiting}
  @{"  Sub-agents and scripts  " link subruns}
  @{"  What the model sees  " link model}
  @{"  Setting it up  " link setup}
@{body}
@endnode

@node answering "Answering a question"
@{h1}Answering a question

The question is a row of the run's status lines, @{tt}Question: <text>@{utt},
on the call that asks. Below it the chat draws a box: the full question, the
options, a text field and @{b}Send@{ub}. A sub-agent's question appears the
same way, in the sub-agent's rows inside the run you watch.

@{table}
The question has | You answer by
no options | typing an answer in the field (@{i}Your answer@{ui}) and pressing Send or Enter
options, one to pick | clicking an option -- it is sent at once, together with whatever is in the field; or typing only, and Send
options, several to pick | ticking the boxes, optionally typing, then Send or Enter
@{body}

Send with nothing ticked and nothing typed sends nothing; the box says
@{i}Write an answer first.@{ui} or @{i}Tick an option or write an answer.@{ui}
The field takes at most 4000 characters.

After sending the box says @{i}Sending…@{ui}, then @{i}Answered: <what you
sent>@{ui}. The row's last line takes the box down in every tab that shows
the run: @{tt}answered by <you>: SQLite -- "for now"@{utt}. With
authentication off the name is @{tt}anonymous@{utt}.

The question, the options and your answer are shown as text, never as
markup, both in the box and in the call's result.

@{h2}In agent-cli chat

The question is printed below the run's status rows: @{tt}? <question>@{utt},
the options numbered, and how to answer. The next line you type answers it:

@{table}
You type | It answers
a number: @{tt}2@{utt} | that option
numbers with commas or spaces: @{tt}1,3@{utt} | those options, where several may be picked
a number, a colon, words: @{tt}2: for now@{utt} | that option, with the words
an option's text: @{tt}SQLite@{utt} | that option
anything else, @{tt}2 days@{utt} too | in your own words
@{body}

Words after a number go with that option only after a colon: without one,
@{tt}2 days@{utt} is your answer, not the second option. Where an option
begins with a number (@{tt}4@{utt}, @{tt}2024@{utt}, @{tt}3 days@{utt}), the
options are lettered instead -- @{tt}a)@{utt}, @{tt}b)@{utt} -- so that
@{tt}4@{utt} is the option 4, never the fourth. A number alone that picks
nothing is taken for a slip; to answer with a number, add a word
(@{tt}5 people@{utt}). The chat repeats what it took:
@{tt}» SQLite: for now@{utt}.

A line that answers nothing -- a number no option has -- is not sent: the
chat says what would answer, puts the line back on the input line, and the
question keeps waiting. Several questions at once (parallel calls,
sub-agents) come one after the other, oldest first: only the one your next
line answers is shown in full, the others are named below it
(@{tt}… then: <question>@{utt}) until their turn. A question that ends
without your answer -- timed out, or the run gave it up -- says so.

A line answers the question that was shown when you began typing it. If that
one ends before you press Enter, the line answers nothing
-- not the next question, which you have not read. A bare number or letter
(@{tt}2@{utt}, @{tt}b@{utt}) means nothing without its question and is
dropped (@{tt}Not taken: <question> -- it no longer waits.@{utt}); anything
else -- an option's text, your words -- goes to the agent as a message. A line you began before any question appeared is such a message
too, as in the web chat below: it ends the question.

@{h2}Writing in the chat instead

In the web chat, you do not have to use the box. A message typed into the chat's normal field
while a question waits ends the question within about a second (the row:
@{tt}not answered here: the user wrote in the chat instead@{utt}), and the
model reads your message as your next one. For a sub-agent's question, see
@{" Sub-agents and scripts " link subruns}.

@{h2}When the answer is refused

@{bullet}@{i}Not taken: No such question is waiting -- it was answered, timed
out or its run ended.@{ui} -- another tab answered first, or the wait is over.
The controls stay off.
@{bullet}@{i}Not taken: Only the user whose run asks, or an admin, may
answer.@{ui} -- you are signed in as someone else.
@{bullet}Any other refusal leaves the controls on for a second try.

@{h2}When nobody answers

@{table}
Case | What happens
no answer within ask_timeout (300 s by default) | the row ends @{tt}no answer within 300 s@{utt}; the model decides itself
the tab was closed and not reopened within gone_after_seconds (15 s) | the question stops: @{tt}not answered, nobody reads the run any more@{utt}
the run is stopped while it asks | the row ends @{tt}not answered, the run was cancelled while the question waited@{utt}; the run stops
the server restarts | the run ends; open questions are not kept
@{body}

A page reloaded while a question waits gets the box back within
@{tt}reask_seconds@{utt} (20 s): the question is sent again that often. A
reload does not count as leaving -- "nobody reads" is counted from the
moment the last tab left.

@{h2}Who may answer

The user the run belongs to, or an admin. Only a person's sign-in counts --
the chat's session or a bearer token -- never an API key: a program that
answers a question put to a person makes the question pointless.

@{b}With authentication off, anyone who reaches the API can answer@{ub} --
the agent itself too, through an HTTP tool or @{tt}curl@{utt} in a shell.
@endnode

@node attended "Who is asked, and when not"
@{h1}Who is asked, and when not

A question is put only where a person can answer it: a run the web chat
started for a signed-in user (or with authentication off), while a tab reads
it, or a turn of @{tt}agent-cli chat@{utt} that shows its status. Everything
else gets the @{tt}unattended@{utt} error at once, without waiting.

@{table}
Started by | Asked?
the web chat, signed in or authentication off, while a tab reads the run | yes
a sub-agent or an agent called as a tool, under such a run | yes, in the stream of the run above it
the web chat, an anonymous visitor with authentication on | no
agent-cli chat, its status shown in colour, typed into a terminal | yes, in the terminal
agent-cli chat with @{tt}--no-status@{utt}, without colour (@{tt}--color never@{utt} or @{tt}text@{utt}, @{tt}NO_COLOR@{utt}, @{tt}TERM=dumb@{utt}), or with input that is not a terminal | no
the tab was closed for gone_after_seconds | no; a waiting question stops
agent-run, a one-shot agent-cli, a run woken in a process of its own (@{tt}--woken@{utt}), the OpenAI-compatible API, a JSON @{tt}/run@{utt}, a job | no
an async sub-agent after the run above it ended | no
a call from a tool_script script, a state machine or a slash command | never -- see @{" Sub-agents and scripts " link subruns}
@{body}

agent-cli chat marks a turn as watched when it shows the run's status lines
and takes the lines typed while the turn runs -- both need a colour terminal
it reads the keys of. Without that, nobody would see the question. A
one-shot @{tt}agent-cli "task"@{utt} is a command, like agent-run: it is not
asked. A turn the chat starts by itself when a run it waited for wakes it is
shown in the same terminal, and is asked like any other -- as a run in an
open tab is.

The web chat marks the runs it starts (@{tt}"attended": true@{utt} on
@{tt}POST /events@{utt}, form field @{tt}attended@{utt} on @{tt}/run@{utt}
with files). Another client that shows its user the stream must send the mark
itself and draw the question from @{tt}meta.ask_user.form@{utt}; see
@{" Setting it up " link setup}.

An agent that has the tool is offered it in every run, watched or not: the
tool list is built per agent, not per run, and a list that changed between
runs would break the prompt cache. One refused call costs less.
@endnode

@node tool "The tool"
@{h1}The tool

One tool, named like the instance: @{tt}ask_user@{utt}. It asks one question
and returns when the question is settled.

@{table}
Parameter | Meaning
question | required. The question, trimmed; at most 2000 characters
options | optional. 2 to 4 texts, each trimmed, not empty, at most 200 characters, no two the same (ignoring case). Left out, @{tt}null@{utt} or @{tt}[]@{utt}: an open question
multi_select | optional, default false. true lets the person pick several options; only with options
@{body}

Bad arguments are refused before anything is asked, with a text that says
what to send instead, for example:

@{code json}
{"status": "error", "error": "options has 5 entries; give 2 to 4, or leave options out and let the user answer in their own words."}
@{body}

@{h2}The answer

@{code json}
{"status": "success", "choices": ["SQLite"], "text": "for now"}
@{body}

@{tt}choices@{utt} are the options picked, exactly as the question lists
them and in its order, whatever order they were clicked in. @{tt}text@{utt}
is what the person typed, trimmed; it may be empty when an option was
clicked. An answer carries at least one of the two. Every other outcome is
listed in @{" What the model sees " link model}.

@{h2}What an answer may be

The route checks every answer against the question: each choice must be one
of its options, more than one only with @{tt}multi_select@{utt}, the text at
most 4000 characters, and not both empty. An answer that does not fit is
refused with 422 and the question keeps waiting. So what reaches the model is
bounded: four options of at most 200 characters and 4000 characters of text.

@{h2}Example

@{code json}
{"question": "The export can go to one CSV per table or one SQLite file. Which do you want?",
 "options": ["One CSV per table", "One SQLite file"]}
@{body}
@endnode

@node waiting "Waiting, and what ends it"
@{h1}Waiting, and what ends it

The call waits until the first of these, and the row's last line says which:

@{table}
Event | Row's last line | Result
the answer | answered by <user>: <choices> -- "<text>" | success, the answer
the person writes into the run instead | not answered here: the user wrote in the chat instead | success with @{tt}replied_in_chat@{utt}
the person writes to a run above (a sub-agent's question) | not answered here: the user wrote to the main conversation | error @{tt}replied_above@{utt}
ask_timeout | no answer within <n> s | error @{tt}timeout@{utt}
nobody reads the run any more | not answered, nobody reads the run any more | error @{tt}gone@{utt}
the run is cancelled (Stop) | not answered, the run was cancelled while the question waited | cancelled
the run is torn down while it waits | question cut off while waiting: <question> | -- (the cancellation goes on)
@{body}

The question is closed before the last line is written: an answer that
comes later is refused (404), never taken for a question already settled. Of
two answers at once, one is taken and the other gets 404.

@{tt}ask_timeout@{utt} is the only limit on the wait. The framework puts no
timeout on a tool call; the watchdog counts steps and thinking, not time;
the LLM timeouts run only while a model call streams. A question may wait
for minutes.

A message typed into the call's own run ends the wait also when it came
while the model was still thinking and has not been read yet: the question
then ends at once, and the model reads the message first.
@endnode

@node subruns "Sub-agents and scripts"
@{h1}Sub-agents and scripts

@{h2}A sub-agent asks

A sub-agent, or an agent called as a tool, runs under a request id that
extends the id of the run that started it. Its status lines reach that run's
stream, and so does its question: the person answers it in the conversation
they watch, in the sub-agent's rows. Whether a person reads is decided by the
run above it that the web chat started.

The person may also type into the main chat instead. That message is for the
run above -- the parent, which sits waiting on the sub-agent -- not for the
sub-agent. So the sub-agent's question ends with @{tt}replied_above@{utt}:
the sub-agent is told it cannot see the message and should finish with what
it has, say what it assumed and return, so the parent can read the message.
A message waiting for a run outside this chain ends nothing.

An @{b}async@{ub} sub-agent keeps asking in the parent's stream while the
parent streams. After the parent's run ended nobody can be asked. While the
parent runs on, a message for it ends the sub-agent's question only if the
parent has not read it yet when the question looks (about once a second).

Which shipped agents may ask is in @{" Setting it up " link setup}; a
sub-agent whose answer a coordinator reads should not
(@{tt}research_worker@{utt} removes the tool with @{tt}!ask_user/*@{utt}).

@{h2}Scripts, state machines, slash commands

Only a call the model makes itself, in its own turn, asks. Such a call
carries the run's cancellation token. A call from a tool_script script, a
state machine activity or a slash command has a status row but no
cancellation token, and its caller would cut the question off with its own
timeout (tool_script: 60 s per call). It is refused at once with
@{tt}not_own_call@{utt}, and its row says so.
@endnode

@node model "What the model sees"
@{h1}What the model sees

The tool description and parameter texts come from the plugin's
@{tt}schema.yaml@{utt}. They tell the model when to ask (the request can be
read in ways that lead to different results, the choice is the user's, a
step is hard to undo) and when not (to confirm, to report progress, for what
its tools can find out), to write a question that stands on its own, and --
when nobody can answer -- to decide, go on and say what it assumed.

@{h2}Results

@{table}
Case | Result
answered | @{tt}{"status": "success", "choices": [...], "text": "..."}@{utt}
wrote in the chat instead | @{tt}{"status": "success", "choices": [], "text": "", "replied_in_chat": true, "note": ...}@{utt}
bad arguments | @{tt}{"status": "error", "error": ...}@{utt}
cancelled | @{tt}{"error": "Tool 'ask_user' was cancelled.", "cancelled": true, "forced": false}@{utt}
all others | @{tt}{"status": "error", "reason": ..., "error": ...}@{utt}
@{body}

The texts, verbatim:

@{table}
Reason | Text
(note) replied_in_chat | A message from the user came in while the question waited; it follows as their next message. Read it -- it may answer the question -- and go on from there.
replied_above | The user wrote to the main conversation instead of answering. That message goes to the agent that started you, and you do not see it. Do not ask again: finish your task with what you have, say in your reply what you assumed, and return, so that agent can read the message.
unattended | Nobody can answer: no person is watching this run (it runs over the API, as a job, or the chat was closed). Do not ask again in this run. Decide yourself, go on, and say in your reply what you assumed.
gone | Nobody can answer any more: the person stopped watching this run while the question waited. Do not ask again in this run. Decide yourself, go on, and say in your reply what you assumed.
timeout | No answer came within <n> seconds. Do not ask the same question again right away: decide yourself, go on, and say in your reply what you assumed -- the user can correct it.
not_own_call | ask_user answers only a call the model makes itself, in its own turn -- not one made from a script or by another tool. Call it directly.
@{body}

The argument errors name what to fix: @{i}question is required: the question
to the user, as text.@{ui}, @{i}options must be a list of short texts (2 to
4), or left out.@{ui}, @{i}an option is empty; every option needs a
text.@{ui}, @{i}two options say the same; every option must be
different.@{ui}, @{i}multi_select needs options to pick from; give 2 to 4
options, or leave multi_select out.@{ui}, and the length limits.

Nothing else reaches the model: the plugin injects no message and changes
no prompt. The person's message written instead of an answer arrives as the
ordinary mid-run user message.

@{h2}Tokens and cache

@{bullet}Tool list: about 440 tokens (cl100k; 1808 characters of JSON) in
the tool list of every agent that allows the tool -- part of the cached
prefix, the same in every run.
@{bullet}Per call: append-only. The call and its result -- a few dozen
tokens, at most about 1000 more for a long typed answer -- land at the end
of the history; nothing earlier is rewritten.
@{bullet}A long wait costs the cache once. The next model call comes when
the person answers; providers keep a prompt cache for minutes (Anthropic: 5
by default, the length of the default @{tt}ask_timeout@{utt}). An answer
after that pays for the whole prefix again.
@endnode

@node setup "Setting it up"
@{h1}Setting it up

The default configuration loads the plugin (@{tt}config/plugins.yaml@{utt}):

@{code yaml}
ask_user:
  type: ask_user
  enabled: true
  config:
    ask_timeout: 300          # seconds a question waits; the only limit on the wait
    reask_seconds: 20
    gone_after_seconds: 15
@{body}

An agent gets the tool through its allowlist:

@{code yaml}
agent_config:
  tools:
    allowed:
      - "+ask_user/*"
@{body}

Shipped with it: @{tt}coder@{utt} (and @{tt}gamedev@{utt}, which inherits
its list), @{tt}sysadmin_agent@{utt} and @{tt}research_agent@{utt} -- agents a
person works with in the web chat. No extra packages.

tool_approval's shipped instance allows @{tt}ask_user/*@{utt}: approving a
question before asking it would ask the same person twice, and a run nobody
watches would get the approval's block instead of "decide yourself". An
agent that switches approvals on keeps that allow rule.

@{h2}Server settings

Under the entry's @{tt}config:@{utt}, read once when the plugin starts. A
value that is not a number above 0 is logged and replaced by the default.

@{table}
Key | Default | Meaning
ask_timeout | 300 | seconds a question waits for an answer
reask_seconds | 20 | a waiting question is sent again this often, so a reloaded page shows it again
gone_after_seconds | 15 | a run nobody has read this long is not asked; a waiting question stops
@{body}

@{h2}The routes

@{code text}
POST /plugins/ask_user/answer    {"question_id", "choices": [...], "text": "..."}
GET  /plugins/ask_user/pending   [?session_id=…&request_id=…]
@{body}

@{tt}/answer@{utt}: 200 taken; 422 an answer that does not fit (the question
keeps waiting); 404 a question that no longer waits; 403 someone else's; 401
no sign-in. @{tt}/pending@{utt} lists the open questions the caller may
answer; @{tt}request_id@{utt} also takes those of the runs under it,
@{tt}session_id@{utt} those asked in the sessions of the agents it called as
tools, at every level.

The question travels as a status line of the call's row, with
@{tt}meta.ask_user@{utt}: @{tt}id, question, options, multi_select,
request_id, session_id, agent, asked_at, expires_at, answer_url@{utt}, and
@{tt}form@{utt} -- the question as every client draws it:
@{tt}prompt, detail, warning, choices@{utt} (each @{tt}value, label@{utt}),
@{tt}multi_select, text@{utt} (@{tt}label, alone@{utt}: whether words answer
without a choice, or null). A client other than the web chat draws the
question from @{tt}form@{utt}, posts the @{tt}value@{utt} of what was picked,
and sends @{tt}attended@{utt} when it starts a run; agent-cli chat does
exactly that, in its own process. For ask_user the choices are the options (value = label),
and words alone answer.

@{h2}Where data lives

Nowhere on disk. Open questions live in the server process; a restart ends
every run that waits.
@endnode
